FAQ = [["What changed in GitHub Copilot during the September 21, 2026 week?","GitHub's September 25 roundup covers four model options—Claude Opus 5.5, GPT-6 Sol, GPT-6 Luna and Grok 4.7—plus local sandboxing, OpenTelemetry, Slack and Teams integrations, JetBrains agent controls and VS Code 1.139 agent sessions."],["Which models were available in the September 21 GitHub Copilot weekly release?","The roundup lists Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Sol for Pro+, Max, Business and Enterprise, and GPT-6 Luna and Grok 4.7 for Pro, Pro+, Max, Business and Enterprise. These are launch-time eligibility details."],["Was Copilot local sandboxing generally available in September 2026?","No. The Copilot app sandboxing announcement on September 23 described a public preview. GitHub subsequently announced general availability of local sandboxing across the Copilot app, CLI and supported VS Code Agent Host sessions on October 7, 2026."],["What changed for Copilot in Slack and Microsoft Teams?","GitHub added model switching during conversations, expanded supported shared context, improved duplicate-issue checks, and made long-running tasks and reconnection behavior more reliable. Details vary by integration."],["What's new for GitHub Copilot in JetBrains and VS Code 1.139?","JetBrains 1.18.0 added preview assisted approvals, message rewinding and shared organization instructions. VS Code 1.139 expanded remote Dev Container agent sessions on SSH, Tunnel and WSL hosts and improved session-list management."],["Do these updates mean Copilot agents are automatically safe?","No. Sandbox and telemetry controls help reduce exposure and improve visibility, but teams still need enforceable permissions, human review, tests, protected secrets and appropriate product-specific configuration."]]
BRIEF = "<section class=\"signal-layout shell\">\n<article class=\"signal-brief\">\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">SXF VERIFIED RELEASE BREAKDOWN · NOVEMBER 8, 2026</p>\n<p class=\"signal-summary\"><strong>GitHub's September 21, 2026 Copilot weekly releases</strong> brought four model options—Claude Opus 5.5, GPT-6 Sol, GPT-6 Luna and Grok 4.7—alongside Copilot app sandboxing and OpenTelemetry, improvements for Slack and Microsoft Teams, JetBrains agent controls, and VS Code 1.139 remote Dev Container sessions. The roundup was <strong>published September 25</strong>; September 21 identifies the release week, not the article's publication date.</p>\n<div class=\"signal-context\"><span>THE KEY DISTINCTION</span><p>This roundup combines separate announcements with different supported plans, app surfaces and release stages. Local sandboxing was a <strong>public preview in September</strong>; GitHub announced its <strong>general availability on October 7, 2026</strong>. The model lists below record launch-time eligibility, not a promise that every model remains available forever.</p></div>\n<div class=\"signal-context\"><span>SXF EDITORIAL VIEW</span><p>The bigger shift is not simply “more models”: Copilot is becoming a multi-surface agent workspace where <strong>model choice, tool permissions, execution isolation and observability</strong> must be assessed separately. The analysis below explains what to test before changing team defaults.</p></div>\n</article>\n<aside class=\"source-card\"><span class=\"source-card-label\">SOURCE OF RECORD</span><strong>GitHub Changelog</strong>\n<p>Original September 25 roundup, with linked primary-source releases and an explicitly labeled SXF analysis.</p>\n<a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-25-github-copilot-weekly-releases-september-21/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Read the original weekly roundup ↗</a>\n<p><small>Primary-source review: November 8, 2026. No independent performance or security testing claimed.</small></p>\n</aside></section>"
BODY = "<section class=\"signal-deep-dive shell copilot-sep21-analysis\" data-sxf-copilot-sep21=\"2026-11-08\" aria-labelledby=\"copilot-sep21-overview\">\n<div class=\"intel-section-head\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">SXF / ORIGINAL TECHNICAL ANALYSIS</p><h2 id=\"copilot-sep21-overview\">GitHub Copilot's September 21 release week, explained beyond the headlines.</h2></div><span>Evidence checked November 8, 2026</span></div>\n<p class=\"copilot-sep21-lead\">If you searched for <strong>“GitHub Copilot weekly releases September 21”</strong> or <strong>“GitHub Copilot September 2026 updates,”</strong> this is the release-by-release explanation: which new AI models were announced, what the Copilot app changed for security and monitoring, and what developers gained in Slack, Teams, JetBrains and VS Code. The release week began <strong>September 21</strong>, and GitHub published its roundup on <strong>September 25, 2026</strong>. Read the <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-25-github-copilot-weekly-releases-september-21/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original GitHub Changelog</a> for the concise announcement.</p>\n\n<div class=\"copilot-sep21-glance\" aria-label=\"Release week at a glance\">\n<div><span>FOUR MODELS</span><strong>Opus 5.5 · GPT-6 Sol · GPT-6 Luna · Grok 4.7</strong><small>Separate paid-plan eligibility</small></div>\n<div><span>AGENT CONTROL</span><strong>Local sandboxing + OpenTelemetry</strong><small>September preview and enterprise telemetry</small></div>\n<div><span>COLLABORATION</span><strong>Slack + Teams</strong><small>Model switching, context, issue-handling fixes</small></div>\n<div><span>DEVELOPER TOOLS</span><strong>JetBrains + VS Code 1.139</strong><small>Approvals, agent history and remote containers</small></div>\n</div>\n\n<nav class=\"copilot-sep21-toc\" aria-label=\"Jump to release details\"><strong>In this breakdown</strong>\n<a href=\"#copilot-sep21-models\">New models & plans</a>\n<a href=\"#copilot-sep21-sandbox\">Local sandboxing</a>\n<a href=\"#copilot-sep21-telemetry\">OpenTelemetry</a>\n<a href=\"#copilot-sep21-collaboration\">Slack & Teams</a>\n<a href=\"#copilot-sep21-jetbrains\">JetBrains</a>\n<a href=\"#copilot-sep21-vscode\">VS Code 1.139</a>\n<a href=\"#copilot-sep21-opinion\">SXF analysis</a>\n<a href=\"#copilot-sep21-faq\">FAQ & sources</a></nav>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-models\">\n<h2>Which AI models were added to GitHub Copilot in the September 21 week?</h2>\n<p>GitHub's roundup groups <strong>four model announcements</strong>. They do not share identical plan eligibility. The following table reflects <strong>what GitHub reported for that launch week</strong>, not a live list of models that every account can select on November 8.</p>\n<div class=\"copilot-sep21-scroll\"><table class=\"copilot-sep21-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Eligible plans in the September roundup</th><th>Practical decision question</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><th><a href=\"/models/claude-opus-5-5/\">Claude Opus 5.5</a></th><td>Pro+, Max, Business, Enterprise</td><td>Does complex, multi-step coding justify higher model spend?</td></tr>\n<tr><th><a href=\"/models/gpt-6-sol/\">GPT-6 Sol</a></th><td>Pro+, Max, Business, Enterprise</td><td>How does it perform on difficult implementation or agent tasks?</td></tr>\n<tr><th><a href=\"/models/gpt-6-luna/\">GPT-6 Luna</a></th><td>Pro, Pro+, Max, Business, Enterprise</td><td>Is it a good default for ordinary development tasks?</td></tr>\n<tr><th><a href=\"/models/grok-4-7/\">Grok 4.7</a></th><td>Pro, Pro+, Max, Business, Enterprise</td><td>Does it handle your coding and reasoning workload reliably?</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>GitHub published <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-22-claude-opus-5-5-is-now-available-in-github-copilot/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">a separate Opus 5.5 release note</a> describing early internal task results and gradual rollout. These are <strong>GitHub's observations</strong>, not independent SXF benchmarks. A model appearing in an announcement does not prove that it is cheaper or better than competitors on a particular repository.</p>\n<p><strong>Availability caveat:</strong> a model's current presence in the picker depends on your Copilot plan, client and organization or enterprise settings. GitHub's <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/ai-models/supported-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">supported-models reference</a> is the correct source for today's availability, while its <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-billing/models-and-pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pricing reference</a> explains AI Credits and token rates. Do not assume a model is free merely because it is selectable, or use September pricing as a November invoice estimate.</p>\n<h3>What should a developer test when switching models?</h3>\n<p>Use the <em>same issue, repository snapshot and acceptance tests</em> to compare two supported models. Record the patch's correctness, unit-test results, time to verified completion, human review corrections and total Copilot usage. A few attractive generated lines of code do not establish a reliable engineering outcome. The <a href=\"/guides/claude-sonnet-github-copilot/\">SXF Copilot/Claude setup guide</a> covers selection mechanics; this story focuses on why the September release week changed the choice set.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-sandbox\">\n<h2>GitHub Copilot local sandboxing: what did the September preview actually restrict?</h2>\n<p>GitHub announced <strong>local sandboxing for the Copilot app on September 23, 2026</strong>. At launch it was a <strong>public preview</strong> designed to constrain commands initiated by an agent. Project-level settings covered extra read/write paths, read-only or denied directories, outbound and local-network access, Git credentials and GitHub CLI credentials. Enterprise settings could impose stricter effective restrictions. These are execution controls, not model intelligence improvements. <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-23-local-sandboxing-in-the-github-copilot-app/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Original preview announcement</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"copilot-sep21-note\"><strong>Important historical correction:</strong> The September 25 roundup describes sandboxing as a preview. On <strong>October 7, 2026</strong>, GitHub separately announced <strong>general availability</strong> for local sandboxing in Copilot CLI, the Copilot app and supported VS Code Agent Host sessions. GitHub says sandboxing is included with Copilot at no extra charge. <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-10-07-local-sandboxing-for-gitHub-copilot-now-generally-available/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">October GA announcement</a>. Both dates matter; replacing the September status without context would misrepresent the original release.</div>\n<p>At the September preview stage, sandboxing was <strong>off by default</strong> for the Copilot app, applied to new sessions when enabled at project level, and did not cover cloud-sandbox or remote-host sessions. The preview also documented failure when an operating system could not enforce the requested sandbox. Teams should check the latest client-specific documentation because scope and setup evolved after launch.</p>\n<h3>SXF security assessment: sandboxing reduces blast radius; it does not verify intent</h3>\n<p>A file restriction can stop an agent from writing to an unauthorized path, but it does not prove that the agent's proposed edit is correct. Network and credential restrictions reduce exposure; they do not replace branch protection, secret scanning, code review or a real test suite. Run dangerous tasks with least privilege, keep sensitive repositories and credentials out of unnecessary context, and do not give an agent broad filesystem access simply to resolve a tool failure.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-telemetry\">\n<h2>OpenTelemetry in the Copilot app: why agent traces matter</h2>\n<p>GitHub added <strong>OpenTelemetry (OTel) configuration through enterprise-managed settings</strong> in a September 22 release. Administrators could export agent activity into compatible monitoring tools, including the sequence of requests to models and the tools the agent called. GitHub's documented setup uses the <code>telemetry</code> property in <code>managed-settings.json</code> and an export endpoint. <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-22-opentelemetry-in-the-github-copilot-app/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub's OTel announcement</a>.</p>\n<p>GitHub says prompt and response content is <strong>excluded by default</strong> from those exports. Teams should still review any capture settings and where telemetry is sent; metadata about repository workflows can itself have operational sensitivity. Capturing traces also does not establish that every action was correct.</p>\n<p><strong>SXF's interpretation:</strong> the pairing of sandbox controls and agent traces is more important than either feature in isolation. One asks <em>“What is the agent allowed to do?”</em> and the other asks <em>“What happened during execution?”</em> A production-ready workflow also needs an answer to <em>“Did the tested outcome match the requested change?”</em> That last question belongs to verification and code review, not telemetry alone. Our <a href=\"/research/ai-agent-reliability/\">AI Agent Reliability research</a> explains how to test repeated success, robustness and failure recovery.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-collaboration\">\n<h2>GitHub Copilot in Slack and Microsoft Teams: more context, fewer duplicate issues</h2>\n<p>The September 25 integration update made Copilot more useful where a ticket often begins: in a team discussion. GitHub described the ability to <strong>switch models during a conversation</strong>, reuse the selected model for the rest of the thread, detect similar issues before creating new ones, and return links connecting created work to its originating conversation.</p>\n<div class=\"copilot-sep21-scroll\"><table class=\"copilot-sep21-table\"><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Announced improvement</th><th>Why engineers should care</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody><tr><th>Slack</th><td>Supported files, attachments and message links as context; model switching; channel workflow improvements</td><td>Reduce manual copying of issue context, with explicit review of scope</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Microsoft Teams</th><td>Inline images, forwarded messages and conversation history; better handling of threads and interrupted tasks</td><td>Make tasks more traceable without losing originating conversation context</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Both</th><td>Similar-issue checks, clearer plan status and reconnect behavior</td><td>Fewer duplicate tickets and easier recovery from long-running sessions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>GitHub's <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-25-updates-to-github-copilot-for-slack-and-microsoft-teams/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">detailed Slack and Teams release note</a> explains the supported context and reliability improvements. Do not assume every message, private file or repository is automatically included: integration authorization and conversation permissions still matter.</p>\n<h3>SXF analysis: conversation-to-code is a provenance problem</h3>\n<p>There is real value in linking a GitHub issue to the message where a bug was reported. The danger is mistaking a fast conversion for an approved requirement. Teams should still require a named issue owner, an explicit acceptance criterion, a repository check and a review of any generated ticket. Duplicate-issue detection should be treated as a suggestion, not a guaranteed deduplication system.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-jetbrains\">\n<h2>What's new for GitHub Copilot in JetBrains 1.18.0?</h2>\n<p>GitHub's September 22 JetBrains 1.18.0 update covered more than model choice. <strong>Assisted approvals</strong>, then in <strong>public preview</strong>, could automatically approve selected low-risk tool calls and still ask for confirmation on higher-risk actions. Editing an earlier user message rewound both conversation and file changes before continuing, giving developers a cleaner way to redirect an agent session. <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-22-new-features-and-improvements-in-copilot-for-jetbrains/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">JetBrains release notes</a>.</p>\n<p>The update also brought organization- and enterprise-shared skills and managed instructions into local and Copilot agent sessions, a planning mode for the Codex agent, and controls for built-in GitHub MCP tools. For a team, those features create a policy surface: review what instructions are shared, which tools are exposed and which approvals can be automated.</p>\n<p><strong>SXF's view:</strong> assisted approvals can reduce interruptions, but “low risk” is contextual. A terminal command may be routine in a disposable test environment yet dangerous against production credentials. Treat approval policy and tool scopes as deployment configuration, not as proof that an agent understands consequences.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-vscode\">\n<h2>VS Code 1.139 Copilot update: remote Dev Containers and agent session management</h2>\n<p><strong>VS Code 1.139</strong> expanded Dev Container agent sessions from local folders to projects hosted on <strong>SSH, Tunnel and WSL</strong>. The remote host needs Docker and a supported Dev Container configuration. The related setting, <code>chat.agentHost.devContainer.enabled</code>, was gradually rolling out; it might not be enabled by default. The release also improved loading for large agent-session lists and added compact and organizational controls. <a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/updates/v1_139\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official VS Code 1.139 release notes</a>.</p>\n<p>For developers with Linux build environments or remote development workstations, executing agent tasks inside the project's actual toolchain can reduce a common source of false confidence: code that passes on the developer's laptop but fails in the target environment. A Dev Container, however, is not automatically a comprehensive security sandbox. Its privileges, mounts, credentials and network access still require review.</p>\n<p>For the broader monthly view across VS Code 1.136–1.140, see <a href=\"/signals/github-copilot-in-vs-code-september-2026-releases-b33dfca/\">SXF's September 2026 VS Code Copilot analysis</a>.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-opinion\">\n<h2>SXF analysis: the real September story is governance across five product surfaces</h2>\n<p>The most important point is not the raw number of model launches. In one week GitHub extended model choice, local agent boundaries, centralized observability, collaboration context and editor-native orchestration. These capabilities address <strong>different failure points</strong> in AI-assisted software engineering. Treating them as one generic “Copilot update” hides the architecture engineers actually need to evaluate.</p>\n<div class=\"copilot-sep21-decision\">\n<article><span>01 · CAPABILITY</span><h3>Which model solves the task?</h3><p>Test comparable tasks with the same files, tools, acceptance criteria and review process. A model picker is a choice mechanism, not a quality guarantee.</p></article>\n<article><span>02 · CONTAINMENT</span><h3>What is it allowed to touch?</h3><p>Apply filesystem, network and credential restrictions before expanding agent autonomy. Ask how policy is enforced, not merely displayed.</p></article>\n<article><span>03 · OBSERVABILITY</span><h3>Can you reconstruct execution?</h3><p>Use agent traces to investigate tool use, interruptions and retries, with deliberate telemetry-data governance.</p></article>\n<article><span>04 · PROVENANCE</span><h3>Where did the task come from?</h3><p>Keep source conversations linked to the created issue or pull request, then verify the human requirement.</p></article>\n<article><span>05 · ENVIRONMENT</span><h3>Where was the code tested?</h3><p>Match remote toolchains and dependencies, and distinguish environment reproducibility from isolation.</p></article>\n</div>\n<h3>A reproducible adoption checklist for engineering teams</h3>\n<ol class=\"copilot-sep21-checklist\">\n<li><strong>Inventory access.</strong> Record plan, supported models, client versions and organization policies; do not assume rollout is identical for all employees.</li>\n<li><strong>Define a task matrix.</strong> Choose small bug fixes, multi-file changes and one constrained agent task; apply the same automated tests to candidate models.</li>\n<li><strong>Enforce boundaries.</strong> Test a denied filesystem path, blocked network request and restricted credential access in a safe environment before trusting a sandbox configuration.</li>\n<li><strong>Trace one complete run.</strong> Confirm that monitoring captures enough data to diagnose model and tool operations without recording confidential prompts unnecessarily.</li>\n<li><strong>Review collaboration handoffs.</strong> Create a sample issue from a controlled Slack or Teams thread; verify repository scope, duplicate suggestions and source attribution.</li>\n<li><strong>Measure the right outcomes.</strong> Compare verified changes, review minutes, failed attempts, end-to-end latency and AI Credits—not generated tokens or marketing claims alone.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"copilot-sep21-note\"><strong>Editorial methodology:</strong> These are SXF's practical recommendations derived from primary-source release descriptions, <em>not</em> results of an SXF benchmark, penetration test, controlled rollout or cost study. Launch-time eligibility and feature states are stated separately from subsequent changes.</div>\n<p>A model's cost and security profile are products of the entire workflow. For deeper model comparison and alternative tool architectures, read <a href=\"/guides/github-copilot-alternatives/\">SXF's GitHub Copilot alternatives</a>, <a href=\"/guides/ai-agent-security/\">AI Agent Security</a> and the <a href=\"/research/ai-agent-reliability/\">agent reliability framework</a>.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-faq\">\n<h2>Frequently asked questions: GitHub Copilot September 2026 releases</h2>\n<h3>What changed in GitHub Copilot during the September 21, 2026 week?</h3><p>The roundup lists four models, Copilot app sandboxing and telemetry, Slack/Teams collaboration improvements, JetBrains controls and VS Code 1.139 remote agent sessions. The roundup itself was published on September 25.</p>\n<h3>Which new Copilot models were listed and for whom?</h3><p>Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Sol were listed for Pro+, Max, Business and Enterprise. GPT-6 Luna and Grok 4.7 were listed for Pro, Pro+, Max, Business and Enterprise. These are September announcement details; confirm live support separately.</p>\n<h3>Is Copilot local sandboxing still in preview?</h3><p>It was a public preview in the September 23 Copilot app announcement. GitHub announced general availability on October 7, 2026 for the Copilot app, Copilot CLI and supported VS Code Agent Host sessions.</p>\n<h3>Can Copilot use Slack messages and Teams context to create issues?</h3><p>GitHub documented supported conversation context, similar-issue checks and direct links from created work to its source discussion. Permissions, integration setup and human review still matter.</p>\n<h3>Does VS Code 1.139 support remote Dev Container agent sessions?</h3><p>Yes, including eligible SSH, Tunnel and WSL project environments; Docker and a supported Dev Container configuration are required on the remote host. GitHub and VS Code described gradual feature enablement.</p>\n<h3>Do sandboxing and OpenTelemetry eliminate agent security risks?</h3><p>No. Sandboxing constrains tool access, while telemetry shows what happened. Neither automatically verifies the resulting code, requirements, secret handling or repository policy.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"copilot-sep21-sources\">\n<h2>Primary sources and verification notes</h2>\n<p>This report is a source-attributed interpretation of GitHub and VS Code release materials. No fabricated performance numbers are used. Release-week facts were cross-checked against their dated announcements; availability and billing should always be rechecked against live GitHub documentation. <strong>Research review: November 8, 2026.</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"copilot-sep21-sources\">\n<li><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-25-github-copilot-weekly-releases-september-21/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub Changelog, September 25: Weekly releases — September 21</a> — primary roundup.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-22-claude-opus-5-5-is-now-available-in-github-copilot/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub, September 22: Claude Opus 5.5 in Copilot</a> — model availability and launch evidence.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-23-local-sandboxing-in-the-github-copilot-app/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub, September 23: Local sandboxing preview</a> — setup and scope at launch.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-10-07-local-sandboxing-for-gitHub-copilot-now-generally-available/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub, October 7: Local sandboxing GA</a> — subsequent status update.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-22-opentelemetry-in-the-github-copilot-app/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub, September 22: OpenTelemetry</a> — traces, managed settings and content-capture defaults.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-25-updates-to-github-copilot-for-slack-and-microsoft-teams/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub, September 25: Slack and Teams update</a> — context and reliability.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-09-22-new-features-and-improvements-in-copilot-for-jetbrains/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub, September 22: JetBrains 1.18.0 update</a> — approvals, skills and agent controls.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/updates/v1_139\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Microsoft VS Code 1.139 release notes</a> — remote Dev Containers and session management.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/ai-models/supported-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub Docs: Supported AI models</a> — current availability.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-billing/models-and-pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub Docs: Models and pricing</a> — per-model usage billing.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/administer-copilot/manage-for-organization/manage-default-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GitHub Docs: Model availability policy</a> — organization and enterprise model controls.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Related SXF analysis:</strong> <a href=\"/signals/github-copilot-weekly-releases-october-5-8e5ec29/\">Copilot October 5 weekly updates</a> · <a href=\"/signals/github-copilot-in-vs-code-september-2026-releases-b33dfca/\">VS Code Copilot September releases</a> · <a href=\"/signals/claude-opus-5-5-is-now-available-in-github-copilot-d6baa9e/\">Opus 5.5 in Copilot</a> · <a href=\"/guides/github-copilot-alternatives/\">Copilot alternatives</a>.</p>\n</section>\n</section>"
